		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Emily Gerrick]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-09-09T08:56:37-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-09-09T08:56:37-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4975#p4975</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4975#p4975"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Item #6 (17 consultant contracts) pulled from consent]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4975#p4975"><![CDATA[
Dear colleagues, <br><br>I requested that item #6 be pulled from the consent agenda tomorrow and wanted to share my thoughts in advance of the meeting. My overall ask is that we vote down this item and ask for it to be brought back in smaller pieces with more specific information. <br><br>Item #6 would authorize 17 contracts for up to $5 million over three years. I’ve met with staff and am informed that the primary motivation for these contracts is to assist with “shared service optimization” in relation to various shared and internal services, and to provide support to Austin Budget and Organizational Excellence for the work ahead. <br><br>I have a few concerns with this item. First, although Council is asked to authorize 17 discrete contracts, the backup for this item does not provide any detail on the individual consultants and the scope of each contract. Second, the item does not provide any guidance on how the $5 million would be allocated among individual contractors. And third, Council is not provided with the projected value or return on investment for individual contracts. <br><br>Previously, in the context of the shared service optimization known as “One ATS” or “One Austin Technology Services,” we heard significant concerns about over-spending on consultants and the quality of advice received and how applicable it is to the City of Austin.  <br><br>My preference for items such as this would be that Council be provided with detail on each proposed consultant contract, and that we have the chance to approve or reject individual contracts instead of being asked for a bulk approval. I also hope that over time, as our City grapples with reduced income from federal contracts and budgetary preemption at the state level, our City management relies less on consulting services and depends more on our salaried City staff. I also believe this approach will provide more transparency to us and to the public than would a blanket approval of $5 million for vaguely defined consultant work. <br><br>Thank you for your consideration and look forward to the discussion Thursday. <br><br>Mike<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1254">Emily Gerrick</a> — Wed Sep 09, 2026 8:56 am — Replies 1 — Views 232</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Max Lars]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-09-01T13:01:23-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-09-01T13:01:23-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4974#p4974</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4974#p4974"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[September 8th Work Session Cancellation]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4974#p4974"><![CDATA[
Council Members--<br><br>The work session scheduled for Tuesday, September 8th will be cancelled since there are no prepared staff briefings, and nothing has been pulled by members of the Council. <br><br>Thanks, <br><br>Kirk <br>On Behalf of Mayor Watson<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1263">Max Lars</a> — Tue Sep 01, 2026 1:01 pm — Replies 0 — Views 1708</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Ryan Alter]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-26T20:17:49-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-26T20:17:49-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4973#p4973</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4973#p4973"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Item 50 - parkland condemnations]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4973#p4973"><![CDATA[
Colleagues,<br><br>Tomorrow I am bringing an amendment to Item 50 (parkland condemnations) to strengthen transparency and council oversight over this process. Specifically, my amendment would:<br><br>- strengthen council oversight overall,<br>- change the duration an impacted park would be governed by this policy from 5 years to 1 year,<br>- change the required deadline for notifying relevant boards and commissions from 90 to 60 days, and <br>- change the value of a park that this policy would apply to from $500,000 to $250,000<br><br>Collectively, these improvements will increase our ability to protect and maintain parkland facing condemnations.<br><br>You can see my full amendment here: <a href="https://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/87-202608238200955.pdf" class="postlink">https://assets.austintexas.gov/austinco ... 200955.pdf</a> <br><br>I look forward to the discussion tomorrow.<br><br>Ryan<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=719">Ryan Alter</a> — Wed Aug 26, 2026 8:17 pm — Replies 0 — Views 1668</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Max Lars]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-26T13:44:36-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-26T13:44:36-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4971#p4971</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4971#p4971"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[8/27 Council Meeting Schedule]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4971#p4971"><![CDATA[
Council: <br> <br>Here is my plan for the Council meeting tomorrow. <br><br>We will begin promptly at 10am. After I call the meeting to order and read the changes &amp; corrections into the record, I plan to begin with an opportunity to recognize and honor Chief Baker as this is his last meeting as our Chief of the Austin Fire Department. Additonally, I plan to pull Item 56 and take it up as our first item which confirms Assistant Chief Tom Vocke as our interim chief.  <br><br>Once we do that, we will then hear public comment on the remaining consent items. Speakers will have 2 minutes per item. Speakers will be able to donate time. Speakers wishing to donate time must coordinate with the City Clerk prior to being called. All parties (speakers and donors) must be present in person to be eligible for donation of time. Speakers will be called to speak on individual consent items in numerical order. Speakers signed up on multiple consent items will have an opportunity to speak on each individual item as the items are called.  <br><br>We will then vote on the consent agenda with brief remarks from Council.  <br> <br>Following the consent agenda,we will then take up any items pulled from consent.The only items that has been pulled at this time is Items 56 &amp; 61.  <br> <br>Following discussion and action on any items pulled, we will take up non-consent items including items referred from committee, eminent domain items, and public hearings. <br> <br>We will hear speakers on public hearings, and non-consent items when the item is taken up. <br> <br>For non-consent items and items pulled from consent, the item will be laid out by staff or the Council Member. We will start with hearing testimony for those items then Council will have the opportunity for a brief discussion to ask clarifying questions.Then we will vote on that item. <br> <br>General Public Communication will begin at 12pm. We will also have live music. <br> <br>At 2pm, we will hear from our zoning consent speakers and vote on the zoning consent agenda. Speakers will have 2 minutes. <br> <br>Should we have zoning items for discussion, we will hear a staff presentation followed by a presentation from the applicant (5 minutes) and from the opposition (5 minutes). We will then hear public testimony. The opposition and the applicant will each have 3 minutes for closing remarks. The Council will then discuss and vote. <br> <br>Thank you, <br> <br>Kirk <br>On behalf of Mayor Watson<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1263">Max Lars</a> — Wed Aug 26, 2026 1:44 pm — Replies 0 — Views 1256</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Krista Laine]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-25T13:11:20-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-25T13:11:20-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4969#p4969</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4969#p4969"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Item #72: Lakeline Station Area Resolution]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4969#p4969"><![CDATA[
Colleagues,<br><br>I would like to bring to your attention Item #72 on Thursday’s regular meeting agenda.  Lakeline Station has carried a Transit-Oriented Development designation since the CapMetro Red Line opened in 2010.  Sixteen years later the area remains significantly underutilized, despite being the only commuter rail station or Park &amp; Ride facility within a five-mile radius, sitting at the intersection of four major corridors with the potential to serve thousands of people a day.  <br><br>Even as new multi-family development and employers have arrived, missing sidewalks and bikeways make it difficult and unsafe to walk or bike around the station.  These gaps have discouraged people from switching to transit, made affordable housing harder to build, and stalled three large approved planned unit developments nearby.  Meanwhile, District 6 has only reached 34% of its ten-year affordable housing goal, and lacks adequate access to public facilities.<br><br>These needs will only grow.  Williamson County’s population is expected to exceed 1.6 million by 2050, and as more residents move to the edges of our city, we need to ensure that they still have access to city services, programs, and public spaces.  Investing in an existing transit hub is smarter and cheaper than building new infrastructure from scratch, and by stimulating development, this investment will expand Austin’s tax base without displacing residents. <br><br>Rather than committing us to any specific plan or financing tool, this resolution directs staff to engage our regional partners, study our options, and develop recommendations for Lakeline Station.  I expect that the lessons we learn here will unlock new approaches in other parts of our city as well.<br><br>I want to thank my co-sponsors, Mayor Pro Tem José "Chito" Vela, Council Member Mike Siegel, and Council Member Paige Ellis, for recognizing this vision early on.  <br><br>I look forward to examining the untapped potential of this part of Austin’s Red Line, and I hope to have your support. <br><br>Thank you,<br>CM Krista Laine<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1145">Krista Laine</a> — Tue Aug 25, 2026 1:11 pm — Replies 1 — Views 1041</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Vanessa Fuentes]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-24T15:50:14-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-24T15:50:14-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4967#p4967</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4967#p4967"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Committed to Banning Large Data Centers]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4967#p4967"><![CDATA[
Colleagues, <br><br>The proliferation of data centers due to AI and cloud computing poses a significant risk to our local community, our resources, and our environment. The five of us are committed to ensuring that Austin does not allow a single large, “hyperscale” data center within the City limits, and we invite all of you to join us in making this commitment.<br><br>This week, we will be discussing and taking action to initiate specific regulations for Data Centers within City Code. This process is necessary before we can impose a formal ban on data centers that are larger than the small-scale facilities used by the University of Texas, banks, hospitals, and other local organizations. We want the public to understand that although Council is initiating Code amendments this week, this process will not allow any large data centers within our City. <br><br>We will use the Work Session on Tuesday to make our intention clear regarding data center policy, including our intention to be as aggressive as possible in preventing large facilities from being built or expanded within Austin. We invite all of our colleagues to join us for a press conference immediately following. Given the significant community interest and regional implications of this topic, we want to elevate the work of our Council on this important issue. <br><br>In Community,<br><br>CM Fuentes<br>CM Velasquez<br>CM Alter<br>CM Siegel <br>CM Qadri<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=380">Vanessa Fuentes</a> — Mon Aug 24, 2026 3:50 pm — Replies 1 — Views 1310</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Max Lars]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-19T17:44:18-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-19T17:44:18-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4962#p4962</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4962#p4962"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Additional protections re: Data Centers]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4962#p4962"><![CDATA[
Council--<br><br>A question has come up about the process going forward now that we have the memo from staff. Please see my previous message board post.<br><br>My intention from the beginning was to create protections and processes in case someone wanted to place a data center in Austin. My strong recommendation is that there be a freeze on approvals of any application to any City department that is for the purpose of placing a data center in Austin city limits, Austin's ETJ, or the service area of an Austin utility until we have a complete policy that has been adopted by Council.<br><br>Thanks, <br><br>Kirk <br>On Behalf of Mayor Watson<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1263">Max Lars</a> — Wed Aug 19, 2026 5:44 pm — Replies 4 — Views 2553</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Max Lars]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-19T12:01:14-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-19T12:01:14-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4961#p4961</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4961#p4961"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Staff Response to Data Centers]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4961#p4961"><![CDATA[
Innovation should align with our community’s values, including the protection of our natural resources and the quality of life of our residents.<br> <br>With this principle in mind, in May, I proposed a framework for preventing and regulating data centers in Austin to assure that the City is a careful and thoughtful steward of our water, energy, and land resources and to prevent incompatible land uses in order to protect our residents. I’m pleased that Council Members Ellis, Alter, Velásquez, and Siegel joined me in this effort. You can read our original post here: <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?t=2256" class="postlink">https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?t=2256</a><br><br>In response to the proposal, City staff released a memo today that will help the City Council establish clear and rigorous protective guardrails that put Austin values first and prevent harm to our community. The memo recognizes the importance of Austin keeping up with the technological changes of our times while protecting our City. The memo:<br><br>•Gives clarity and increases transparency on existing data centers currently operating in Austin.<br><br>•Provides staff recommendations on policies to prevent, limit, regulate, and manage data centers. This includes a new set of land use requirements and restrictions specifically targeting high impact data centers, directives for new permitting protections, and mechanisms for identifying and strengthening environmental sustainability performance standards for high use customers of Austin Energy and Austin Water.<br><br>•Renews our commitment to the economic development framework Council unanimously passed in May, which offers a clear vision on how we build and deploy our economic development tools, so that companies meet the standards Austinites want and expect.<br><br>•Includes other appropriate and relevant mechanisms using those tools currently accessible to local government to curb unsuitable uses. <br><br>I appreciate the professionalism and thoughtfulness from our City staff. You can read the memo here: <a href="http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/0C-202608231112335.pdf" class="postlink">http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincou ... 112335.pdf</a><br><br>Now it is time for the City Council to act deliberately and decisively. We will do that. <br><br>On August 25, Austin City Council will hold a Work Session focused on data centers. We will hear from staff about the mechanisms available to us. On August 27, Council will have discussion and possible action related to data centers. <br><br>The concerns arising from rapid development of high impact data centers across our country and its impact on our local utilities, water supply, and use of land are legitimate. These concerns also present an opportunity for us to set the standard of what a responsible future looks like.<br><br>Thanks, <br><br>Kirk <br>On Behalf of Mayor Watson<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1263">Max Lars</a> — Wed Aug 19, 2026 12:01 pm — Replies 0 — Views 2100</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Jose Chito Vela]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-12T11:21:38-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-12T11:21:38-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4959#p4959</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4959#p4959"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[D4 Budget Amendments/IFCs]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4959#p4959"><![CDATA[
Dear Colleagues, <br><br>As I shared last Thursday, I am bringing forward two budget amendments and two IFCs:<br><br>1. IFC #1 (Version 2): Directs the City Manager to assess the cost to develop and implement a publicly accessible database for G-file data. (<a href="http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/A3-202608224110437.PDF" class="postlink">http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincou ... 110437.PDF</a>)<br><br><br>2. IFC #2 (Version 2): Directs the City Manager to study if the proposed increase in the drainage charge rate for Austin Watershed Protection is sufficient for the City’s drainage infrastructure needs and bring back required ordinance if necessary to increase the change in the proposed drainage utility charge. (<a href="http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/A3-202608224110246.PDF" class="postlink">http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincou ... 110246.PDF</a>)<br><br><br>3. Budget Amendment #1 (Version 2): Amends the Transportation and Public Works capital budget to add $32 million in funding for safety and active mobility projects. (<a href="http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/A3-202608224111848.pdf" class="postlink">http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincou ... 111848.pdf</a>) <br><br><br>4. Budget Amendment #2: Amends the Austin Animal Services capital budget by adding $3m for construction, renovation, and expansion improvements to Austin Animal Services facilities. (<a href="http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/A3-202608224111908.pdf" class="postlink">http://assets.austintexas.gov/austincou ... 111908.pdf</a>) <br><br>Looking forward to a productive conversation on the dais. <br><br>Saludos, <br><br>Chito<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=679">Jose Chito Vela</a> — Wed Aug 12, 2026 11:21 am — Replies 0 — Views 1738</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[Julie Montgomery]]></name></author>
		<updated>2026-08-12T10:02:33-05:00</updated>

		<published>2026-08-12T10:02:33-05:00</published>
		<id>https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4958#p4958</id>
		<link href="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4958#p4958"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Item 17 v3 - Parks Security IFC]]></title>

					<category term="City of Austin Council Message Board" scheme="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewforum.php?f=2" label="City of Austin Council Message Board"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://austincouncilforum.org/viewtopic.php?p=4958#p4958"><![CDATA[
On behalf of CM Ellis:<br><br>Thank you, CM Siegel, for your amendment to my Parks Security resolution. I believe the original resolution prohibits the use of AI cameras by requiring full compliance with the TRUST Act, but I'm happy to have it made explicit. Please see a v3 of my resolution that incorporates your amendment. The Agenda Office will be distributing this v3 shortly.<br><br><a href="https://assets.austintexas.gov/austincouncilforum/A0-202608224095944.pdf" class="postlink">https://assets.austintexas.gov/austinco ... 095944.pdf</a><br><br>Thanks,<br>Paige<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://austincouncilforum.org/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=320">Julie Montgomery</a> — Wed Aug 12, 2026 10:02 am — Replies 0 — Views 1607</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
